## 1. Introduction
Supercapacitor is a type of new energy-saving and conversion equipment with a high-power density and rapid discharge–charge rate [1–3]. Various materials such as carbon-based materials, transition metal oxides, and transition metal sulfides have been widely studied as supercapacitor's electrode materials to improve its performance and satisfy people's higher and higher demands [4–6]. In comparison with transition metal oxides or hydroxides, transition metal sulfides have lower electronegativity and higher electrochemical activity, which could accelerate electron transport and promote the redox reaction in pseudo-capacitor [7–9]. In particular, Ni-Co-based sulfides can provide a generous redox reaction due to the coexistence of Co and Ni ions instead of corresponding single metal sulfides [10,11]. These properties make Ni-Co-based sulfides exhibit excellent conductivity and expected redox activity, which has been widely concerned in the field of supercapacitors. Yang et al. decorated edge site-enriched Ni-Co-S nanoparticles on graphene frameworks (Ni-Co-S/G). The Ni-Co-S/G hybrid electrodes exhibited a high specific capacitance of \(1492\mathrm{F~g^{-1}}\) at 1 A \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) ascribed to the Ni-Co-S edge sites with high electrochemical activity and a strong affinity for \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) in the electrolyte [12].
However, the rate performance and the cycle performance significantly attenuate for the pure metal sulfide-based electrodes during the electrochemical testing process. Combining them with conductive carbon frameworks is an effective strategy to increase active sites furtherly and achieve better electrochemical performance by providing more conductive channels and lowering powder materials accumulation in related fields [13–15]. Huang's group synthesized Ni-Co-S nanosheets on graphene foam by a simple one-step electrochemical method. The composites can provide a large effective area for redox reactions and facilitate the diffusion of the ions, which exhibited a high specific capacitance value of \(2918\mathrm{F~g^{-1}}\) [16]. Carbon nanotubes (CNTs) are typical and widely used carbon material for designing electric double-layer capacitors electrodes due to their good mechanical strength, high specific surface area, and considerable electrical conductivity [17,18]. More importantly, CNTs can form interconnected conductive porous network structures to shorten the electron and ion transport paths [19,20]. Liu and coworkers prepared vertically aligned Ni-Co sulfide nanowires on carbon nanotube fibers through an in-situ two-step hydrothermal reaction method. Due to its rich Faradic redox reaction sites and superior electric conductivity, the \(\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\) CNT electrode exhibited a high volumetric capacitance of 2332F \(\mathsf{c m}^{-3}\) [21]. MOFs (metal organic frameworks) derivatives have many advantages, such as rich pore structure, adjustable elements, controllable structure, etc., which are ideal precursors for preparing multi-metal sulfides [22,23]. Therefore, we infer that high performance electrode materials can be obtained by combining MOFs with carbon nanotubes and subsequent in-situ vulcanization, and to our knowledge, there are few systematic studies in this area.
In this work, carbon nanotubes with high electrical conductivity are selected to interpenetrate MOFs-derived Co-Ni-S composite spheres and build an 3D interconnected architecture. Such a collaborative strategy can form mesoporous channels for facile electron transfer and provide more pseudocapacitive. Owing to the synergistic effect of each component, where carbon nanotubes contribute to electron conduction and cycle stability and Co-Ni-S is responsible for high capacity as-prepared Co-Ni-S/CNTs elec
trode presents a high specific capacitance of \(540.6\mathrm{C~g^{-1}}\) at 1 A \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) and a satisfactory capacity retention rate of \(69.9\%\) from 1 A \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) to \(10\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) . Furthermore, the as-assembled hybrid supercapacitor, with Co-Ni-S/CNTs as the positive electrode and activated carbon as the negative electrode, respectively, exhibits a high energy density of \(63.5\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1}\) at \(800\,\mathsf{W}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1}\) . Moreover, the supercapacitor shows considerable cycling stability with \(83.0\%\) initial capacitance retention after 10,000 cycles. This synthesis strategy provides a new pathway for further designing carbon nanotubes-based composite electrodes for future energy storage.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All chemicals used in the experimental section were analytical grade and without further purification. \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.\) - \(\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM), methanol, and ethanol were purchased from Aladdin. Carbon nanotubes aqueous dispersion (CNTs, \(1.5\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) ) were purchased from XFNANO Company Limited.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Co-Ni-S/CNTs composites
Typically, \(0.88\mathrm{~g~Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(0.8\,\mathrm{~mL~}\) CNTs dispersion were added into \(20\;\mathrm{mL}\) methanol (named mixture A), respectively. \(1.60\ \mathrm{g}\) of 2-MIM was dissolved in \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) of methanol and then slowly poured into mixture A, following by sonication for \(2~\mathrm{min}\) . The mixture was aging in room-temperature for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . The obtained sample (named as Co-MOF/CNTs) was washed with ethanol several times and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . \(0.02\mathrm{~g~Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was dissolved in a mixture of \(25~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol and \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water. The prepared Co-MOF/CNTs precursor was added into the above solution and reacted in an oil bath at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{3\mathrm{~h~}}\) , resulting in CoNi-LDH/ CNTs. \(0.24\mathrm{~g~Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was dissolved in \(30~\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water, following the adding of as-prepared CoNi-LDH/CNTs and reaction for \(^{3\mathrm{~h~}}\) at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Finally, the obtained sample (Co-Ni-S/CNTs) was washed with ethanol several times and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Co-Ni-S (without adding CNTs), \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{S}_{4}/\mathsf{C N T s}\) (without Ni ion exchanging) and \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{S}_{4}\) (without Ni ion exchanging and adding CNTs) were prepared for comparison.
## 2.3. Material characterizations
X-ray diffractometer (XRD, XRD-6100 Shimadzu Instruments Co., Ltd) with Cu Pd \(\mathfrak{x}\) radiation was used to characterize the material composition of the sample. The surface morphology of the electrode was identified by a field emission SEM (FESEM, Japan Hitachi S-4800). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM, JEM2010HR) were employed to examine the structure and morphologies' intrinsic features. Oxidation states of the elements and surface chemical composition were measured by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo ESCALAB 250XI Thermo Fisher Scientific).
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurement
The performance of all the electrodes was evaluated by an electrochemical station (CHI 760E) at room temperature. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves, galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) curves, and the electrochemical impedance spectrometry (EIS) analyses were carried out to study the electrochemical performance. The testing environment is a beaker-type three-electrode system with the prepared samples employed as a working electrode, platinum wire as a counter electrode, and \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) as a reference electrode. The working electrode preparation process is as follows: samples, acetylene black, and polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) were mixed with an 8:1:1 mass ratio on a piece of Ni foam
\((1.0\,\mathrm{cm}\times1.0\,\mathrm{cm})\) as the current collector. 6 M KOH aqueous solution was used as an electrolyte throughout the test. All the electrochemical measurements of as-assembled supercapacitors were performed in a two-electrode system, where activated carbon was used as the negative electrode.
## 3. Results and discussion
The preparation processes of Co-Ni-S/CNTs were shown in Fig. 1. Firstly, \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) was mixed with CNTs and dissolved in a methanol solution of 2-MIM to obtain regular polyhedral materials of ZIF-67 connected with CNTs as a bridge. Then, \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) was
used to substitute partial Co ions at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to obtain CoNi-LDH/ CNTs. Finally, Co-Ni-S/CNTs composite electrode was obtained by further vulcanization using \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}\).
The morphologies and microstructures of CoNi-LDH/CNTs and Co-Ni-S/CNTs are investigated by SEM and TEM, as shown in Fig. 2. Fig. 2a is the surface structure of CoNi-LDH/CNTs, where the CNTs connect the regular polyhedron particles as bridges. This structure is beneficial to increase the conductivity of the material. After vulcanization, the edges and corners of the polyhedral structure become smooth and the shape changes to spheres, but CNTs are still embedded in the structure, as show in Fig. 2b. Fig. 2c and d are the TEM images of Co-Ni-S/CNTs with different magnification. From Fig. 2e it can be seen that the Co-Ni-S composites show fluffy structure, which is conducive to the diffusion of electrolyte in the active material and provides more active sites for electrochemical reaction. From the partially enlarged TEM view in Fig. 2d, CNTs extend from the outer structure, indicating CoNi-S is closely connected with carbon tubes, and such structure can increase the conductivity of the electrode. Fig. 2e is an HRTEM image that displays the clear lattice fringes of \(0.25\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(0.28\;\mathrm{nm}\) corresponding to the (0 2 1) plane of NiS and (3 1 1) plane \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\). Fig. 2g-j are the elemental distribution mappings of Co-Ni-S/CNTs material, indicating the uniform distribution of Ni, Co, S from CoNi-S spheres and C from CNTs.
Fig. 3a demonstrates the XRD pattern of the Co-Ni-S/CNTs composites. The detected diffraction peaks at \(18.4^{\circ}\), \(30.4^{\circ}\), \(32.4^{\circ}\), \(35.8^{\circ}\), \(40.6^{\circ}\), \(57.6^{\circ}\), \(59.8^{\circ}\) and \(67.5^{\circ}\) match well with (1 1 0), (1 0 1), (3 0 0), (0 2 1), (2 1 1), (3 3 0), (0 1 2) and (6 0 0) planes of NiS (JCPDF NO. 86–2280). The diffraction peaks located at \(26.9^{\circ}\), \(31.5^{\circ}\), \(38.2^{\circ}\) and \(50.4^{\circ}\) are well indexed to (2 2 0), (3 1 1), (4 0 0), and (5 1 1) planes of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) (JCPDF NO. 74–0138). The diffraction peak at \(23.3^{\circ}\) is the characteristic peak of carbon nanotubes. The above results prove the successful preparation of Co-Ni-S/CNTs composite. XPS further explored the chemical valence states of the synthesized Co-Ni-S/ CNTs sample. The XPS survey spectrum in Fig. 3b indicated that Co-Ni-S/CNTs composites are mainly composed of Co, Ni, S, and
C. High-resolution Co 2p XPS spectrum in Fig. 3c is fitted into two vibration peaks of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) and two vibration orbits (represented as "sat"). The peaks at \(781.86\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(797.9\;\mathrm{eV}\) are corresponding to \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and the peaks located at \(779.5\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(796.8\;\mathrm{eV}\) are attributed to \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) [24,25]. The spectrum of Ni 2p is also fitted into two kinds of vibration peaks of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\). The binding energies at \(856.0\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(857.4\;\mathrm{eV}\) correspond to Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\), while the peaks centered at \(873.7\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(875.6\;\mathrm{eV}\) belong to Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\), respectively [26,27]. The C 1s spectrum of the composite is fitted with three peaks corresponding to \(\mathrm{C-C=O}\), \(\mathrm{C-O}\), and \(\mathrm{C-C}\) in Fig. 3e. The peak area of \(\mathrm{C-C}\) is much higher than that of \(\mathrm{C-O}\) and \(\mathrm{O-C=O}\), showing the efficient removal of oxygen-containing groups during oil bath treatment, which is conducive to improve the conductivity of carbon nanotubes [28,29]. The S 2p spectrum in Fig. 3f is fitted into two vibration peaks, \(162.4\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(163.7\;\mathrm{eV}\), representing S \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and S \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\), respectively [30].
The electrochemical properties of the obtained electrodes employed \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{CNT}\), Co-Ni-S, and Co-Ni-S/CNTs were first tested using a three-electrode system. Fig. 4a shows the CV curves of the three electrodes at a scanning rate of \(2\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). The redox peaks appeared in both positive and negative scanning regions of the four curves, and the peak area of Co-Ni-S/CNT is larger than that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{CNT}\) and Co-Ni-S electrodes, indicating its higher capacitance. Fig. 4b shows the GCD curves of the three electrodes under the voltage of \(0{-}0.5\;\mathrm{V}\) at the current density of \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The apparent platform corresponds to the redox peak in CV curve. Co-Ni-S/CNT electrode shows the longest charge and discharge time, indicating its largest capacitance. Fig. 4c shows the CV curves of Co-Ni-S/CNT-based electrode at different scanning rates, where all the curves have a pair of symmetric redox peaks, indicating that the redox reaction occurred at the corresponding position. The reaction process is shown below [31,32].
\[
\mathrm{Co}_3\mathrm{S}_4+\mathrm{OH}^-\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Co}_3\mathrm{S}_4\mathrm{OH}+e^-
\]
\[
\mathrm{Co_3S_4OH+OH^{-}\leftrightarrow Co_3S_4O+H_2O+e^-}
\]
\[
\mathrm{NiS+OH^{-}\leftrightarrow NiSOH+e^{-}}
\]
\[
\mathrm{NiSOH+OH^{-}\leftrightarrow NiSO+H_2O+e^{-}}
\]
Fig. 4d is the GCD curves of Co-Ni-S/CNTs-based electrode at different current densities. The charge and discharge shapes are consistent at various current densities, and the specific capacitance can still maintain \(69.9\%\) when the current density is 10 times higher, indicating its good rate properties. Fig. 4e shows the
specific capacity comparison of the three electrodes at different current densities. Obviously, the specific capacity of Co-Ni-S/CNT-based electrode is the largest. The calculated specific capacitance of Co-Ni-S/CNT-based electrode are \(540.6\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), \(519.3\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), \(507.2\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), \(468.0\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), \(414.2\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and \(378.0\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), \(2\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), \(3\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), \(5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), \(8\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and \(10\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. At a current density of \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), the specific capacity of the Co-Ni-S/CNTs-based electrode is about 2.5 times larger than that of the Co3S4/CNTs-based electrode and 5 times larger than that of the Co3S4-based electrode. The Co-Ni-S/CNTs-based electrode has the best electrochemical properties due to the following reasons:
Firstly, the carbon nanotubes are evenly interpenetrated in Co-Ni-S spheres resulting in an 3D interconnected architecture, which can improve the electron/ion transfer rate in the whole composite material. As shown in Fig. 4e, the rate performance also increases from \(48.6\%\) to \(69.9\%\), which indicates that the conductivity of our electrode material is indeed improved, leading to the increase of rate capability. Secondly, Co-Ni-S spheres have a fluffy structure, which benefits the rapid diffusion of electrolytes and increases the effective contact area between active materials and electrolytes, further accelerating ions' migration. As shown in Fig. 4f, Co-Ni-S/CNTs-based electrode shows a line almost vertical to the real axis, indicating the fluffy structure is beneficial to accelerate the electrolyte flow dynamics within the electrode. Thirdly, the synergistic effect between Ni and Co bimetallic sulfides can provide adequate active sites for redox reactions. To further compare and analyze the electrochemical differences among the four materials, an EIS test was conducted with insets showing the equivalent circuit diagram, as exhibited in Fig. 4f. All the curves show an unobtrusive semicircle in the high-frequency range and a line at the low-frequency range [33]. The semicircle diameter shows the resistance for charge transfer, and the line's slope is related to the resistance of ion diffusion [34,35]. After adding carbon nanotubes, the composites of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{CNTs}\) and Co-Ni-S/CNTs show lower impedance than \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) and Co-Ni-S/CNTs, indicating CNTs can enhance the electron conductivity. The semicircle diameter of the Co-Ni-S/CNTs-based electrode is the smallest, indicating the smallest internal resistance \((1.732\ \Omega)\) and highest electron conductivity. The results suggest that compounding with carbon nanotubes and exchanging with nickel ions can synergistically enhance active components' electron conductivity. Moreover, Co-Ni-S/CNTs-based electrode shows a line almost vertical to the real axis, indicating the fluffy structure is beneficial to accelerate the electrolyte flow dynamics within the electrode. Besides, the cycle experiment of Co-Ni-S/CNTs is further supplied in Fig. S2, after 3000 cycles, the capacity can still keep \(85.3\%\) of the original value. The FE-SEM image of Co-Ni-S/CNTs after cycling test was also
provided. As shown in the inset of Fig. S2, the carbontubes and nanoparticles can still be observed connected together, so our electrode materials have good stability.
The electrochemical behavior of the Co-Ni-S/CNTs-based electrode was further studied using the CV test at the potential window \((0{-}0.5\ \mathrm{V})\) and scan rates on a big scope \((2{-}50\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})\), as presented in Fig. 5a. All the curves have the same shape under different scanning rates. The electrochemical process is divided into two behaviors, which is battery behavior and pseudo-capacitance behavior. The energy storage mechanism of the electrode is studied by analyzing the scan rate and the peak current response. The relationship can be expressed as follow formula:[36]
\[
I=a\nu^{b}
\]
The b value is relevant to the charge storage dynamics. In our experiment, the b values of the anodic and cathodic are 0.6869 and 0.7113, suggesting battery and pseudo-capacity coexisting in Co-Ni-S/CNTs-based electrodes.
The capacitance contribution is calculated using the formula as follows:[37]
\[
I/\nu^{0.5}=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{0.5}
\]
where \(\mathbf{k}_{1}\) and \(\mathbf{k}_{2}\) are related to the current contributions. The CV curves at the scan rates of \(2.0\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), \(5\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), and \(50\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) are presented in Fig. 5c-e, which exhibit \(45.0\%\), \(49.1\%\), and \(71.2\%\) capacitive effects to the total specific capacitance, respectively. Fig. 5f shows that the capacitive contribution increases following the increase of scan rates, which is attributed to the improved speed of charge/electron diffusion and the decreased ion diffusion path.
To further test the practical application of Co-Ni-S/CNT composite, the hybrid supercapacitor device is fabricated using Co-Ni-S/CNTs and activated carbon electrodes as positive and negative electrodes, respectively (Fig. 6a). Fig. 6b shows the CV curves of activated carbon and Co-Ni-S/CNTs based electrode under their respective potential windows at the scanning rate of \(2\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\).
The working voltage window for the Co-Ni-S/CNTs electrode is \(0-0.5\,\mathrm{V}\) and for the activated carbon electrode is \(-1.0-0\,\mathrm{V}\). Hence, the working voltage window of the Co-Ni-S/CNT//AC supercapacitor can expand to \(1.5\,\mathrm{V}\). Fig. 6c shows the CV curves of the supercapacitor under different potential windows. There is no polarization phenomenon in the range of \(0-1.6\,\mathrm{V}\), but polarization can be observed when the voltage window is larger than \(1.6\,\mathrm{V}\) \((0-1.8\,\mathrm{V})\). During all electrochemical performance tests of the two-electrode device, the working voltage is set between 0 and \(1.6\,\mathrm{V}\). The CV curves and GCD curves were tested under this working voltage and shown in Fig. 6d-e. When the scanning rate increases
from \(2\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) to \(100\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), the shape does not change significantly, indicating its good charge and discharge performance. In addition, from the GCD curves in Fig. 6e, we can see that the charge and discharge curves have good symmetry, and there is no polarization phenomenon, indicating that the supercapacitor can work stably within \(0-1.6\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage window. The good symmetry of the curves indicates that the device has good electrochemical performance and the Coulombic efficiency can reach \(86.6\%\) at \(1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Based on the result of GCD curves in Fig. 6e, the Co-Ni-S/CNTs//AC supercapacitor shows specific capacitances of 178.6, 158.7, 152.2, 135.1, 110.1, and \(99.5\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at current densities of 1, 2, 3,
5, 8, and \(10\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\), respectively. The 10000-cycle endurance test was carried out under \(10\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) current density to test the device's stability in Fig. 6f. The capacity of Co-Ni-S/CNTs//AC was maintained at \(83.0\%\), indicating its good stability.
The energy density/power density of supercapacitor is an important parameter for measuring its practical application performance. The Ragone plot of the Co-Ni-S/CNTs//AC supercapacitor was showed in Fig. 7. The as-fabricated device exhibits a high energy density of \(63.5\mathrm{~Wh~kg^{-1}}\) at power density of \(800\mathrm{~W~kg^{-1}}\), which is competitive with reported nickel–cobalt sulfide-based hybrid supercapacitor, such as \(\mathrm{CoNi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) NSs/NCF//AC (42.8 Wh \(\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) at \(400\mathrm{~W~kg^{-1}}\)) [38], \(\mathrm{CoNi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{AC}\) (33.9 Wh \(\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) at \(409\mathrm{~W~kg^{-1}}\)) [39], \(\mathrm{CoNi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\mathrm{-rGO}\) /porous carbon (51.7 Wh \(\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) at \(762\mathrm{~W~kg^{-1}}\)) [40], \(\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) nanotubes/NGF//AC (36.8 Wh \(\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) at \(375\mathrm{~W~kg^{-1}}\)) [41], \(\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{rGO}\) //AC (27.5 Wh \(\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) at \(446.5\mathrm{~W~kg^{-1}}\)) [42], \(\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{rGO}\) //AC (49.1 Wh \(\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) at \(375\mathrm{~W~kg^{-1}}\)) [43]. The illustration in Fig. 7 is an application example of lighting a pentagram (12 LEDs) with two devices in series, indicating its practical application.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, an interconnected architecture with carbon nanotubes interpenetrating MOFs-derived Co-Ni-S composite spheres had been successfully synthesized for supercapacitor electrode. The employment of conductive carbon nanotubes "bridges" between Co-Ni-S composite spheres can significantly lead to favorable electronic structures for fast charge transfer and significantly optimize the capacitive utilization. Benefiting from the synergistic effect of the two components, the Co-Ni-S/CNTs based electrode exhibits abundant electroactive spots, shortens ion transport pathways, and improves redox reaction kinetics. Therefore, the Co-Ni-S/CNTs based electrode showed a remarkable capacitance and rate performance due to its good electronic/ionic conductivity. The hybrid supercapacitor's superior energy density demonstrates its competitive candidate for future application and development of supercapacitors.
